{
  "image_name": "1df80bdeed837ac3be8897752ff1ac54.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two distinct mitochondrial cell-death pathways by comparing a normal steady state with stressed states. In the upper half, apoptosis is shown as mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization (MOMP). Under steady conditions, BAX and BH3-only proteins are separated and BAK1 is restrained at the outer membrane, apparently by BCL-2 family control. During MOMP, BAX and BAK1 assemble into pores in the outer mitochondrial membrane, allowing cytochrome c to escape into the cytosol, which is linked to apoptosis.\n\nThe lower half shows mitochondrial permeability transition (MPT) and regulated necrosis. In the resting state, the permeability transition pore complex (PTPC) in the inner membrane is closed, with PPIF present in the matrix. In the activated state, the PTPC opens, leading to dysregulated influx of ions and water across the inner membrane. This is associated with swelling-like changes and is tied to regulated necrosis rather than apoptosis.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that mitochondria can trigger different death programs through different membranes and pore systems: BAX–BAK1 pores in the outer membrane for cytochrome c release and apoptosis, versus PTPC opening in the inner membrane for ion imbalance and necrotic death.",
